<Writeup
  title="Guitar Trainer"
  description="A dependency-free fretboard trainer that listens through the mic: autocorrelation pitch detection accurate to ~1 cent, a Karplus-Strong synth, and six practice modes with rhythm scoring."
  meta="Personal project · In active development"
  tags={['JavaScript', 'Web Audio', 'DSP']}
  links={links}
>
  <p>
    Fretboard drills only work if something is actually checking you. Guitar Trainer listens through
    the microphone, hears which note you played, and advances automatically when you're right — no
    clicking between reps, no taking your hands off the guitar. It's a single static page: no
    frameworks, no libraries, no server.
  </p>

  <h2>The listening part</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Pitch detection</strong> — autocorrelation with parabolic interpolation, written in
      plain JavaScript against the Web Audio API, accurate to about one cent across the guitar's
      range. A live tuner shows the cents meter and signal strength.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Sound synthesis</strong> — prompts and ear-training tones are generated with
      Karplus-Strong plucked-string synthesis, so the app sounds like a guitar instead of a sine
      beep. It also self-mutes while playing so it doesn't detect its own output.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Six practice modes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Single notes</strong> — find a named note on a named string against the clock; a focus
      mode weights the drill toward your historically slowest positions.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Scale runs</strong> — major, minor, pentatonics, blues, and arpeggios from a given
      position, optionally against a metronome with on-beat scoring.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Ear training</strong> — hunt a played pitch by ear, with difficulty tiers from "reference tone + string name" to "any octave, no hints." Miss patterns are logged by interval.</li>
    <li><strong>Call &amp; response</strong> — transcribe 2–5 note phrases back note-for-note.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Bends &amp; holds</strong> — bend to a target pitch or hold a note within ±20 cents
      for three seconds; pure intonation training.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Daily workout</strong> — a fixed circuit chaining the other modes, with a summary
      comparing today against previous sessions.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Everything persists to local storage — response times, mistakes, per-string averages, miss
    patterns — and renders as a <strong>fretboard heatmap</strong> of average find-times by
    position, so the neck itself shows you what to practice next.
  </p>
</Writeup>
